\begin_layout Title
Will Posting Salary Information Have an Effect on Company Profits or Productivit
y?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
February 4, 2014
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For many individuals, the topic of yearly salary is taboo.
 Proper social etiquette dictates that asking about a person's salary is
 off-limits because such personal information should be kept private.
 A majority of private companies in the United States follow this norm,
 yet they have different reasons for doing so.
 They believe that posting salary information may cause problems in the
 workplace.
 The posting of salaries could cause strife between the upper echelon of
 earners and those individuals earning a substantial amount less.
 Yet, not only could conflicts arise, but it may give their employees the
 wrong incentives.
 For example, employee A is making $45,000 per year with no opportunity
 for a raise or advancement in the near future, while a comparable associate
 is making $47,000 per year.
 If employee A obtains the information concerning the differences in salaries,
 then he may have an incentive to reduce his productivity and effort to
 the level which prevents him from get fired and/or reprimanded by his superiors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

However, there are still many companies who are electing to eliminate the
 moratorium on salary disclosure.
 First off, employees talk with one another, so salary information is not
 totally private.
 By making this information readily available, it facilitates a level of
 trust between employees.
 Furthermore, keep salaries private perpetuates unfair salaries, which could
 be detrimental for employees and the organization.
 If salaries are kept completely private, then employees feelings of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

unfairness
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 are unable to be addressed directly.
 Finally, companies claim that releasing salary information increases the
 transparency of the company to customers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

So, posting salaray information could have an effect on a company's productivity
 and/or profits.
 In order to to estimate this effect, I propose a differences-in-differences
 estimation procedure.
 In fact, there can be two ways to approach this investigation.
 First, I can analyze the data of entire companies who elected to begin
 posting salary information.
 Obviously, I would obtain data for a reasonable number of time periods
 before and after treatment.
 The control group would be companies in the same industry, or a comparable
 one, as those companies in the treatment group, who have elected not to
 change their salary policies.
 Second, if companies with frachise locations, such as Subway, McDonald's,
 etc., allow store managers and/or owners to decide their own policies concerning
 salary, then I can compare data from individual stores who have elected
 to disclose salary information against those who have not.
 As stated before, the outcomes I would measure would be the productivity
 and/or profits of a specific company or an individual store.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The identifying assumption of difference-in-difference is that absent the
 treatment, the change in the outcome for the treatment and control groups
 would have been the same over time.
 Alternatively, absent the treatment, the difference between the treatment
 and control groups would have remained the same in the post-period as in
 the pre-period.
 If I control for the following fixed effects: individual company (or store)
 fixed effects, industry, and region, then I expect the identifying assumption
 will hold.
 At least for the second procedure, it is not a drastic stretch to assume
 that individual frachise stores in the same region would, in general, experienc
e the same types shocks, so the treatment and control groups should track
 each other fairly well over time.
 Thus, any difference in profit and/or productivity between the treatment
 and control group can be attributed to the difference in salary policity.
 Admittedly, showing that the identifying assumption holds in the first
 procedure could be somewhat problematic.
 As a result, I would have to perform extensive robustness checks in order
 to determine if my proposed treatment and control groups track each other
 fairly well in the pre-period.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Currently, several private-sector firms have already began revealing details
 of company financials, staff performance reviews, and individual pay.
 Companies such as SumAll, Buffer, Tenmast Software, and Peer Insight are
 now posting salary information.
 These companies can serve as my treatment group.
 Now, what about the control group? Well, one possibility is to construct
 a synthetic control from a combination of other companies that do not reveal
 personal salary information and that operate in the same industries as
 the four firms just listed.
 I realize that it would improve my empirical strategy to expand my treatment
 group by searching for companies that have begun to post individual pay.
 However, this data could be difficult to obtain since companies do not
 often publicly announce such drastic policy changes.
 Though, The Center for Effective Organizations at USC may provide me with
 the additional information that I am currently seeking.
\end_layout

